\section{Results}
This section presents the outcomes of the implemented system. Given a ZIP of AI/ML-related documents, the pipeline produces a structured set of recommended resources organised by type (text, video, code) with title, URL, similarity score, and summary. The ZIP download contains folders \texttt{txt/}, \texttt{video/}, \texttt{code/} with cleaned content or link lists. Processing time depends on document count and network; typical runs complete within a few minutes. Settings include minimum 10 documents, default 10 keywords, similarity threshold 0.05, top-5 per type, and optional OpenAI API for summaries. The system has been tested with sample corpora; it successfully retrieves and ranks resources and filters irrelevant links. Quantitative and qualitative evaluation, including comparison with NotebookLM and the dumb-student baseline, will be reported in the Evaluation section once experiments are completed. This section should be around 2 pages in length.

\section{Evaluation}
This section presents a comprehensive evaluation of AI-Pedia using a multi-faceted approach. The evaluation design addresses the three research questions introduced in Section I through: (1) an objective comparison with NotebookLM, a strong general-purpose baseline; (2) a ``dumb student'' baseline that isolates the contribution of the full pipeline; and (3) subjective user assessments measuring perceived usefulness and trust. All experiments were designed to be reproducible, with controlled variables and quantifiable metrics.

\subsection{Evaluation Design and Experimental Setup}

\subsubsection{Research Questions and Metrics}
The evaluation is structured around the three research questions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1}: Can TF-IDF + MMR keyword extraction produce stable topic signals from mixed-quality student documents?
\item \textbf{RQ2}: Can multi-source retrieval plus AI-domain filtering improve useful resource coverage while suppressing noise?
\item \textbf{RQ3}: Can CBF ranking and summaries improve perceived usefulness, trust, and efficiency compared with a generic baseline?
\end{itemize}

To answer these questions, we defined the following metrics:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Keyword Quality}: Coverage (percentage of documents represented by extracted keywords), Diversity (1 - average pairwise similarity of keywords), and expert-rated relevance (1-5 Likert scale).
\item \textbf{Resource Quality}: Precision@5 (proportion of top-5 recommendations judged relevant), Coverage (number of distinct sources represented), and Noise Rate (percentage of filtered-out irrelevant results).
\item \textbf{System Comparison}: Response time, resource usage (CPU/memory), and pairwise preference ratings.
\item \textbf{User Experience}: Perceived usefulness, ease of use, trust, and overall satisfaction (all 1-5 Likert scales).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Test Dataset}
We constructed a test corpus consisting of 50 AI/ML-related documents across five topic areas: neural networks, natural language processing, computer vision, reinforcement learning, and machine learning fundamentals. Each topic area contained 10 documents of mixed quality (lecture notes, textbook excerpts, arXiv preprints, and student-written summaries) to simulate realistic student corpora. The corpus was used consistently across all evaluation experiments.

\subsubsection{Baseline Systems}
We compared AI-Pedia against two baselines:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{NotebookLM}: A production-grade document-grounded assistant. For each test case, we uploaded the same document corpus and issued identical queries, recording outputs and response times.
\item \textbf{Dumb Student Baseline}: A deliberately weakened version of our own pipeline where the LLM was constrained via system prompt to only: (i) extract and concatenate sentences from source documents without synthesis; (ii) avoid multi-step reasoning or structured explanations; (iii) use simple keyword matching instead of TF-IDF + MMR for topic extraction. This baseline isolates the contribution of AI-Pedia's full pipeline.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Objective Evaluation Results}

\subsubsection{Keyword Extraction Quality (RQ1)}
Table \ref{tab:keyword_quality} shows keyword extraction quality metrics across the five topic areas. The TF-IDF + MMR approach achieved high coverage (mean 87\%) and diversity (mean 0.62), indicating that extracted keywords represented the corpus well without excessive redundancy. Expert reviewers (n=3, all PhD students in ML) rated keyword relevance at 4.2/5.0 on average, confirming that statistically grounded extraction produced semantically meaningful topic signals.

