The largest single contributor was CBF ranking (removal reduced Precision@5 by 29\%), followed by AI-domain filtering (-18\%). MMR contributed primarily to diversity (+29\% improvement). LLM summaries had minimal impact on objective metrics but improved user satisfaction in subjective evaluation (see below).

\subsection{Subjective Evaluation Results}

\subsubsection{User Study Design}
We recruited 24 participants (undergraduate and master's students in Computer Science) for a within-subjects user study. Each participant completed learning tasks using all three systems (AI-Pedia, NotebookLM, Dumb Student) in counterbalanced order to control for learning effects. After each condition, participants completed a questionnaire measuring:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Perceived Usefulness}: ``The resources recommended by this system were useful for my learning.''
\item \textbf{Ease of Use}: ``It was easy to get the information I needed from this system.''
\item \textbf{Trust}: ``I trust the recommendations provided by this system.''
\item \textbf{Overall Satisfaction}: ``I am satisfied with this system's performance.''
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Questionnaire Results}
Table \ref{tab:subjective_results} presents the subjective evaluation results. AI-Pedia received significantly higher ratings than both baselines on all dimensions (p < 0.05, repeated-measures ANOVA with post-hoc Tukey HSD tests). The largest difference was in Perceived Usefulness (4.3 vs. 3.4 for NotebookLM and 2.8 for Dumb Student), suggesting that learners valued the targeted external resource recommendations.

