\subsubsection{Qualitative Feedback}
Open-ended responses revealed several themes:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Resource Discovery}: Participants appreciated that AI-Pedia ``found resources I wouldn't have discovered on my own'' and ``connected my notes to external explanations.''
\item \textbf{Transparency}: Several users noted that the CBF similarity scores helped them ``understand why a resource was recommended.''
\item \textbf{Time Savings}: Participants reported spending less time searching and more time learning: ``I used to spend 30 minutes finding resources; now it's 2 minutes.''
\item \textbf{Limitations}: Some users wanted more fine-grained control over filtering thresholds and requested support for additional resource types (e.g., podcasts, interactive tutorials).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Threats to Validity}

We acknowledge several limitations:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sample Size}: The user study involved 24 participants from a single institution, which may limit generalizability.
\item \textbf{Task Coverage}: Evaluation tasks focused on AI/ML topics; results may differ for other domains.
\item \textbf{NotebookLM Configuration}: We used default NotebookLM settings; different configurations might yield different results.
\item \textbf{Temporal Validity}: External resources (especially YouTube and GitHub) may change or become unavailable over time.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Summary of Evaluation Findings}

The evaluation provides evidence supporting all three research questions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1}: TF-IDF + MMR produced stable, high-quality keyword extractions (87\% coverage, 0.62 diversity, 4.2/5.0 expert rating).
\item \textbf{RQ2}: Multi-source retrieval with AI filtering improved resource coverage while reducing noise (34\% filter rate, 0.72 Precision@5).
\item \textbf{RQ3}: CBF ranking and the full pipeline improved both objective metrics and subjective user experience compared to baselines (68\% user preference, 4.2/5.0 satisfaction).
\end{itemize}

The ablation study confirmed that each pipeline component contributes meaningfully, with CBF ranking and AI filtering being the most critical for recommendation quality. The ``dumb student'' baseline demonstrated that the full pipeline adds substantial value beyond simple document-grounded retrieval.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper presented AI-Pedia, an AI-powered multimedia resource recommendation system for machine learning education. The system addresses a key gap in existing AI learning tools: while products like NotebookLM excel at document-grounded explanation and summarisation, they provide limited support for structured reinforcement learning workflows that require discovering external resources across heterogeneous platforms.

AI-Pedia's core contribution is a learner-driven reinforcement pipeline that transforms a student's private AI/ML materials into curated external resource recommendations. The system combines established information retrieval and recommender system methods (TF-IDF, MMR, CBF) with multi-source retrieval from Wikipedia, Google Scholar, arXiv, YouTube, and GitHub. The evaluation demonstrated that this approach improves recommendation relevance (Precision@5: 0.72 vs. 0.58 for NotebookLM), resource diversity (4.2 vs. 2.1 distinct sources), and user satisfaction (4.2/5.0 vs. 3.5/5.0).

The ``dumb student'' baseline confirmed that the full pipeline contributes substantially: removing TF-IDF + MMR, AI-domain filtering, and CBF ranking reduced Precision@5 by 43\%. The ablation study further showed that CBF ranking and AI filtering were the most critical components, while MMR primarily improved keyword diversity.

Key lessons learned during this project include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Modularity enables evaluation}: Designing the system as a modular pipeline allowed us to isolate and measure the contribution of each component through ablation studies.
\item \textbf{Domain filtering is essential}: Open web search returns substantial noise; the AI-domain filter removed 34\% of candidates while preserving relevant resources.
\item \textbf{Transparency builds trust}: Providing similarity scores and source attribution helped users understand and trust recommendations.
\item \textbf{Fallback mechanisms improve robustness}: The rule-based summary fallback ensured system functionality even when LLM APIs were unavailable.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}

We acknowledge several limitations of this work:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Evaluation scope}: The user study involved 24 participants from a single institution; larger-scale studies across diverse populations would strengthen generalizability.
\item \textbf{Domain specificity}: The system was designed and evaluated for AI/ML education; adaptation to other domains would require curating domain-specific keyword lists and potentially adjusting filtering rules.
\item \textbf{Resource volatility}: External resources (especially YouTube videos and GitHub repositories) may change or become unavailable, affecting recommendation stability over time.
\item \textbf{Computational cost}: The full pipeline takes approximately 2 minutes to process a typical corpus, which may be prohibitive for real-time applications.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Future Work}

Several directions for future work emerge from this project:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Expanded evaluation}: Conduct a longitudinal study tracking learning outcomes over weeks or months, comparing students who use AI-Pedia with those who rely on manual search or generic assistants.
\item \textbf{Domain adaptation}: Extend the system to support additional domains (e.g., mathematics, physics, programming) by curating domain-specific keyword lists and training custom filtering models.
\item \textbf{Enhanced personalization}: Incorporate learner modeling to adapt recommendations based on prior knowledge, learning goals, and preferred learning styles.
\item \textbf{Interactive refinement}: Allow users to provide feedback on recommendations (e.g., ``more like this'' or ``less like this'') and use this feedback to refine future suggestions.
\item \textbf{Resource freshness monitoring}: Implement periodic re-validation of recommended resources to detect and replace broken links or outdated content.
\item \textbf{Integration with learning platforms}: Explore integration with existing learning management systems (e.g., Canvas, Moodle) or note-taking tools (e.g., Notion, Obsidian) to embed AI-Pedia into existing student workflows.
\end{enumerate}

In conclusion, AI-Pedia demonstrates that a reinforcement-oriented recommendation system can meaningfully support AI/ML learners by connecting their private study materials to high-value external resources. The evaluation results suggest that the system's modular, interpretable pipeline offers advantages over both generic document-grounded assistants and unguided manual search. While limitations remain, the project provides a foundation for future work on personalized, domain-aware educational recommendation systems.

